The test runner compiles actual production bodies from sle_conn.rs,
sparklink_core.rs and sle_usb_ffi.c. Only dependency/container/USB types are
fixtures. Production source hashes are recorded and checked again after tests.

No test sender or success injection is compiled into the kernel. Fixture
acceptance proves a submission/accounting boundary, not negotiated transport,
socket ABI, peer delivery, hardware or RF. The native WS73 discovery path must
also be regressed with a freshly built kernel and two real devices in a VM.

The Rust source is compiled with -D warnings. C boundaries require
ASan/UBSan/LeakSanitizer; a restricted ptrace environment may prevent LSan
execution and must be corrected, not recorded as a passing/skipped gate.

Optional query-recipe CBMC check (no device access):

  python3 tools/testing/selftests/sparklink/run_link_query_cbmc.py --output NEW_DIRECTORY

This compiles the actual production connection.c separately from
connection/link_query_cbmc.c, checking private query selectors4/5, arbitrary
32-bit request fields, 64-bit lengths, valid objects/NULL, output guards,
failure atomicity, exact LE16 and recipe/validator agreement. Unwind64 has
unwinding assertions. The runner requires all ten named properties and all
generated safety properties, and two deliberately wrong private source copies
must produce the expected counterexamples. Missing/unsupported/timeout/warning
or incomplete results remain UNPROVED. No production source is modified.

CBMC/frontend/solver and serialized C object semantics are trusted. This does
not prove Host locking, generation/lifetime, Grant/credit/Ticket, callbacks,
NOCP/reuse, firmware behavior, application delivery or implementation refinement.
test_link_query_cbmc.py checks verdict parsing only; its success is not a proof.
